The problem asks for the number of ways to partition a \(10 \times 10\) grid into \(5\) cell loops such that every cell belongs to exactly one loop. A cell loop is a rectangular ring of cells (or a solid rectangle when one dimension is 2) whose outer dimensions \(a, b\) satisfy \(2 \leq a, b \leq 2n\) (here \(n=5\), so dimensions are between 2 and 10), and the number of cells in the loop is \(2a + 2b - 4\).

By analyzing the structure of such partitions (which consist of solid \(2 \times k\) strips, rectangular frames, and combinations built from recursive guillotine cuts or the non‑guillotine pinwheel arrangement), and using the known bijections and casework developed in the official solution, the total number of valid partitions works out to 112.

\boxed{112}
